vm-inline-primitives — inline the hot arithmetic/comparison/cons primitives as byte-code ops
[plan-2, the real lever for call-bound compiled code]

MOTIVATION (measured — see the vm-9 benchmark + the fast-apply finding)
  The byte-code VM is ~9.8x faster than the tree-walker on operation-bound bodies (tight
  arithmetic loops) and ~31x on CLOS dispatch, but only ~1x on CALL-bound code (naive fib,
  tak) and ~0.7x on a tight cons loop (revbuild).  Fast-apply (binding operand-stack args
  straight into a compiled callee, no arg-list cons) was implemented (v1.8.19) and is
  correct, but moved the call-bound numbers only ~2-4% (within noise).  The benchmark and
  the analysis agree on WHY: bocl's per-call cost is CPU-bound, not allocation-bound, AND
  the hot calls in call-bound code are to BUILT-INS — `+ - * < = ` in fib/tak, `cons` in
  revbuild — which fast-apply does NOT touch (it only skips the arg list for COMPILED
  callees; a built-in still gets an arg LIST built + a kernel_apply dispatch, because
  built-ins consume a Lisp arg list by their C calling convention).

  So the remaining lever for call-bound code is to stop routing the most common primitives
  through (build-arg-list -> kernel_apply -> C builtin -> teardown) and instead execute them
  as dedicated byte-code opcodes that read their operands straight off the operand stack.

WHAT
  - Add byte-code ops for the hottest fixed-arity primitives: BC_ADD2 BC_SUB2 BC_MUL2
    BC_LT2 BC_GT2 BC_LE2 BC_GE2 BC_NUMEQ2 (2-arg), BC_CONS, BC_CAR BC_CDR (1-arg), maybe
    BC_ADD1/BC_SUB1 (1+/1-), BC_EQ/BC_EQL.  Each pops its operands and pushes the result;
    BYTECODE_VERSION bump (append opcodes, never renumber — vm-8 fasls stay loadable).
  - Codegen (vm-compiler.lisp): when a CALL is to one of these symbols, the callee is
    fboundp to the ORIGINAL kernel primitive, and the call is not shadowed/traced, emit the
    inline op instead of CALL_NAMED.  MUST be sound: only inline when the symbol's function
    is the built-in (a user (defun + ...) or a later redefinition must fall back to
    CALL_NAMED) — check identity at compile time and/or guard.  Preserve full semantics
    (bignum overflow, type errors, the numeric tower) by having the inline op call the same
    C arithmetic entry the builtin uses, just without the arg-list/kernel_apply wrapper.
  - Keep the fallback: variadic / &rest / traced / redefined uses stay CALL_NAMED.

VALIDATE
  Full 42-suite green; re-run tests/vm-benchmark.lisp — fib/tak should move well above 1x,
  revbuild above 1x (its cons loop becomes BC_CONS).  Confirm bignum + type-error semantics
  unchanged (a suite run with GC/bignum paths).

DEPENDS ON: vm-4 (codegen), vm-9 (done).  Fast-apply (v1.8.19, done) is complementary but
independent — it helps compiled->compiled calls; this helps the far more common
compiled->builtin calls.
